<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
	<meta charset="utf-8">
	<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
	{#- Titles and descriptions come from imported content, which contains real
	    ampersands. They are piped through `safe` wherever a template computes
	    them into a string (eleventyComputed re-renders that string as Nunjucks,
	    so an un-`safe`d value gets escaped once on the way in and again here —
	    which is what turned every "Tax & Advisory" into "Tax &amp; Advisory" on
	    screen). Here the value is raw, so normal autoescaping is correct. -#}
	{#- The brand is appended here, once, instead of being written into every
	    title. It used to be hardcoded as " - Prosperity Partners" in nine
	    templates (index.njk used " | ") and baked into 41 of the 55 page
	    records — so the 14 records without it shipped bare titles, and the
	    firm's name lived in 50 places.

	    The test is containment rather than a per-template opt-out flag: a title
	    that already names the firm (the homepage's
	    "Prosperity Partners | Accounting, Tax & Sage Intacct Solutions", or
	    home.md's "Prosperity Partners LLC") is left exactly as written. -#}
	<title>{{ title }}{% if global.site_name not in title %} - {{ global.site_name }}{% endif %}</title>
	<meta name="description" content="{{ description }}">
	{#- Every page's canonical is derived from its own URL — no template sets one.
	    It used to be emitted from a `canonicalUrl` that only four templates set,
	    so 327 of 373 pages shipped href="", which is invalid and resolved
	    inconsistently by crawlers. Two templates went on setting it by hand to
	    exactly what this produces, with the domain written out a second time;
	    deriving it here means a new template cannot reintroduce either. -#}
	<link rel="canonical" href="{{ global.site_url + page.url }}">
	{#- The archives render visible Newer/Older links but emitted no relation in
	    <head>, so nothing told a crawler that /culture/page/2/ is a continuation
	    of /culture/ rather than a separate page with the same subject. -#}
	{%- if pagination.href.previous %}
	<link rel="prev" href="{{ global.site_url }}{{ pagination.href.previous }}">
	{%- endif %}
	{%- if pagination.href.next %}
	<link rel="next" href="{{ global.site_url }}{{ pagination.href.next }}">
	{%- endif %}

	{#- article for a post, profile for a person, website for everything else.
	    All 374 pages used to say website, including the 104 posts that carry a
	    publication date the filters below already know how to format. -#}
	<meta property="og:type" content="{{ ogType or 'website' }}">
	<meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ global.site_name }}">
	<meta property="og:title" content="{{ title }}">
	<meta property="og:description" content="{{ ogDescription or description }}">
	<meta property="og:url" content="{{ global.site_url + page.url }}">
	<meta property="og:image" content="{{ ogImage or (global.site_url + '/assets/video/home-poster.jpg') }}">
	{%- if ogPublished %}
	<meta property="article:published_time" content="{{ ogPublished }}">
	{%- endif %}

	<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
	<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
	<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Urbanist:ital,wght@0,200;0,400;0,700;1,400&display=swap">
	<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/css/style.css">

	<link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="32x32">
	<link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="32x32" href="/favicon-32x32.png">
	<link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="16x16" href="/favicon-16x16.png">
	<link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="192x192" href="/android-icon-192x192.png">
	<link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="180x180" href="/apple-icon-180x180.png">
	<link rel="manifest" href="/manifest.json">
	<meta name="theme-color" content="#ffffff">
</head>
<body>
	<a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>

	{% include "header.njk" %}

	{#- data-pagefind-body scopes the search index to page content. Once any page
	    carries it, Pagefind indexes only pages that do — so `noIndex: true` in a
	    template's frontmatter is enough to keep that page out (used by the
	    archive/directory pages, whose card lists would otherwise match every
	    query and outrank the actual record). The chrome inside it (banner nav,
	    CTA, filters) is excluded with data-pagefind-ignore at each source.

	    data-pagefind-filter records each page's content type so a search can be
	    narrowed to one type. The team directory's "Search members" box does
	    exactly that (?type=personnel) — the theme scoped it with a hidden
	    post_type=personnel input, and without the filter it returns whole-site
	    results. -#}
	<main id="main"{% if not noIndex %} data-pagefind-body data-pagefind-filter="type:{{ searchType or 'page' }}"{% endif %}>
		{{ content | safe }}
	</main>

	{% include "footer.njk" %}

	<script src="/assets/js/main.js" defer></script>
	{#- Turnstile's widget script. The .cf-turnstile div in contact-form.njk is
	    inert without it — the div alone renders nothing, which is how the forms
	    ended up with no captcha at all. Gated on a configured key because a
	    placeholder key makes the widget render a visible error instead of a
	    challenge; set turnstile_site_key in data/global.json to switch it on. -#}
	{% if global.turnstile_site_key and global.turnstile_site_key != "TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY" %}
	<script src="https://challenges.cloudflare.com/turnstile/v0/api.js" async defer></script>
	{% endif %}
</body>
</html>
